KZ2 patch 6107: every read-mostly SPU gate in a section of its own, so MinGW's GNU ld resolves the weak gates.

Root cause of the 2026-11-26 regression (every exe from main at ~3 fps, every native SPU job falling back).
The gates in runtime/spu/spu_context.h are weak definitions (SPU_GATE), one copy per translation unit,
and KZ3 patch 1102 put them all in .data$kz3rm. A weak definition on a PE target is a weak external
whose default is a per-object symbol; GNU ld (MinGW, binutils 2.46 here) resolves that default at twice
its offset in its section. Only the gate at offset 0 of a TU's .data$kz3rm was read where it lives; the
others read and wrote the words past them (another gate, the next TU's copy or padding). Before patch
7045 that happened to land on words the lifted code's own probes had settled; 7045's ninth gate
(g_spu_ls_dbg_off) moved the layout, spu_ls_dbg_armed read words that are never settled, so every LS
access of every lifted function took the gated path and every native SPU gate fell back
(Gfx.Post.DepthOfField slot 1 calls=1198 fallback=1197).

SPU_READ_MOSTLY now names one section per definition (.data$kz3rm<N>, N from __COUNTER__): each gate is
at offset 0 of its own section, where the default resolves right, and the linker still sorts all of
them between 1102's .data$kz3rl and .data$kz3rn fences. g_spu_irqwait_diag, the one SPU_GATE outside
the group, joins it for the same reason. Same variables, same values; non-PE builds are unchanged.
-DSPU_NO_READ_MOSTLY brings the old layout back, and with it the mis-resolution of the weak gates.
Host test: tools/runtime/test_spu_gate_weak_pe.sh (MinGW link of three TUs with this header: every
TU's reference to each gate resolves to one address that is a gate's own default; the header with
this patch reversed is the negative control).

--- a/runtime/spu/spu_context.h
+++ b/runtime/spu/spu_context.h
@@ -664,7 +664,15 @@
  * and .data$kz3rn bound the group, so no other variable shares its lines. -DSPU_NO_READ_MOSTLY = the old layout
  * (A/B; set it for every runtime TU). Not a semantic change: same variables, same values. */
 #if defined(_WIN32) && defined(__GNUC__) && !defined(SPU_NO_READ_MOSTLY)
-#  define SPU_READ_MOSTLY __attribute__((section(".data$kz3rm")))
+/* KZ2 patch 6107: one section per definition (.data$kz3rm<N>, N = __COUNTER__; still sorted between the fences).
+ * GNU ld (MinGW, binutils 2.46) resolves a weak definition's per-object default at twice its offset in its section:
+ * with every gate of a TU in one .data$kz3rm, the gates after the first read and wrote the bytes past them (another
+ * gate, another TU's copy or padding). Patch 7045's ninth gate moved them so that spu_ls_dbg_armed read unsettled
+ * words: every LS access took the gated path and every native SPU job fell back (~3 fps). At offset 0 the default
+ * resolves right. Host test: tools/runtime/test_spu_gate_weak_pe.sh. */
+#  define SPU__RM_STR2(x) #x
+#  define SPU__RM_STR(x) SPU__RM_STR2(x)
+#  define SPU_READ_MOSTLY __attribute__((section(".data$kz3rm" SPU__RM_STR(__COUNTER__))))
 #else
 #  define SPU_READ_MOSTLY
 #endif
@@ -1167,7 +1175,8 @@
  * job manager waiting on an event) reports what it is waiting on every 2^22
  * such dispatches. g_spu_irqwait_diag is -1 until the first call reads the
  * variable, then 0/1, so with it off the dispatch loop pays one load. */
-SPU_GATE int g_spu_irqwait_diag = -1;
+/* KZ2 patch 6107: in a section of its own (SPU_READ_MOSTLY), so a GNU ld PE link resolves the weak default right. */
+SPU_GATE SPU_READ_MOSTLY int g_spu_irqwait_diag = -1;
 void spu_irqwait_diag(spu_context* ctx);
 
 /* Drain a translated call up to its explicit architectural return PC. */
